Subject: [PATCH v2 0/3] regulator: add the WL2868C camera PMIC and wire up its
 users on the Lenovo Legion Tab

This series adds support for the Will Semiconductor WL2868C, an I2C
camera power management IC with seven LDOs, together with its in-tree
users: the regulator driver (patch 2) and the board description of the
Lenovo Legion Tab (TB322FC, SM8750) that wires the camera devices to
the LDO outputs (patch 3).

The DT binding (patch 1) is unchanged from the v2 that was posted
standalone on 28 October, which already carries the fixes from that
round (subject prefix, MAINTAINERS placement). The driver and the
board description are new to this series; the series carries v2 so
that its version history stays consistent with the binding.

Two notes on where this version came from:

  - the binding commit message of the first posting contained a
    duplicated paragraph; all three commit messages here are written
    fresh for this series.

  - the reviews asked who actually uses the binding. The answer is now
    part of the series: patch 3 describes the board wiring, and the
    consumers are these three devices on the WL2868C outputs

      - rear camera, samsung,s5kjns: dvdd -> LDO1, avdd -> LDO4,
        dovdd -> LDO7
      - front camera, galaxycore,gc08a8: dvdd -> LDO2, avdd -> LDO3,
        dovdd -> LDO7
      - rear VCM, giantec,gt9764: vdd -> LDO5, vddio -> LDO7

    LDO1 to LDO5 and LDO7 are all in use; LDO6 is not wired out on
    this board.

The driver is a plain regulator driver: regmap over I2C, one
regulator_desc per LDO registered with devm_regulator_register(),
linear voltage mapping, no private sysfs or debugfs interface. SYS_EN,
the chip-wide enable, has no consumer of its own; the driver sets it
together with the per-LDO bit on enable and only clears the per-LDO
bit on disable, so that turning one rail off cannot gate the chip
itself.

Patch 3 also adds the two CCI I2C controllers to the SM8750 dtsi,
because the camera devices hang off them. The sensors are described
with their supplies only for now; their CSI endpoints will come with
the camera subsystem work.

